  "response": "## What the scanner says about `src/assets/check-icon.png`\n\nFinding `crime_00040` — charge **\"Icon-Sized Raster\"** (`raster_should_be_vector`, severity `low`, confidence `0.85`):\n\n> \"32×32 PNG icon-sized raster. SVG would scale cleanly across DPIs and ship smaller; the current asset locks the image to a single resolution.\"\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - \"dimensions: 32 × 32 px (≤ 64 threshold both sides)\"\n> - \"format: png\"\n> - \"consider replacing with an SVG icon — same render at every DPI, smaller bytes\"\n\nFix shape: `\"ship the SVG source; rasters lose at any scale\"`. Effort: `quick`. Suggested action `switch_to_svg_icon`, risk `low`.\n\n## Why a new SVG indicator beats another PNG at 32×32\n\n**32 × 32 is exactly the size where raster buys you nothing.** The detector's threshold (≤ 64 px on both sides) encodes the reason: at icon scale the artwork is a handful of simple shapes, so the vector description is *smaller in bytes* than the pixel grid, not larger. The usual argument for PNG — photographic detail that vectors can't express — doesn't apply to a check mark or a status dot.\n\n**The 32 px is a hard ceiling, and you'll hit it.** As the summary puts it, the asset \"locks the image to a single resolution.\" A status indicator will get rendered at 16 px in a dense table, 24 px in a list row, 32 px in a header, and 2×/3× of all of those on retina and mobile displays. With a PNG that's an @2x/@3x export ladder per size — every one a separate file to keep in sync — and anything above 32 px is upscaled and soft. The SVG is one file that renders sharp at all of them.\n\n**Status indicators are multi-state, which multiplies the raster cost.** An indicator usually needs green/amber/red/neutral at minimum. In SVG that's `fill=\"currentColor\"` and a CSS class per state — one asset. In PNG it's states × DPI variants, and the color lives inside the binary where no theme token can reach it. Dark mode makes this worse, not better.\n\n**Agent-legibility, which is the part `crimes` actually cares about.** A PNG is an opaque blob: nothing in the repo, and no agent editing it, can see what it depicts or diff a change to it. An SVG is text — reviewable in a PR, greppable, editable without a design tool round-trip. The existing PNG's `agent_risk` is only `0.05` because one stale icon is cheap; the cost is that copying its pattern means every future indicator inherits the same blind spot.\n\n## One trap to avoid\n\nDon't produce the SVG by wrapping a PNG export. The same scan flags `crime_00038` on `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` — charge **\"SVG With Embedded Raster\"**, severity `high`, confidence `0.95`:\n\n> \"2 embedded base64 rasters inside the SVG (image/jpeg, image/png). The SVG mime type promises infinite scale, but the actual pixels are locked to one resolution — usually larger than a plain PNG.\"\n\nThat's a strictly worse outcome than the PNG you started with — it carries all the resolution lock-in plus ~33% base64 overhead, while looking correct by file extension. The new indicator needs real vector geometry (`<path>`, `<circle>`), re-exported from the design source, not a raster in an SVG envelope.\n\n**Recommendation:** author the new indicator as a vector SVG with `currentColor`, and re-export `check-icon.png` from the same source while you're in there — it's a `quick`, `low`-risk change per the scanner, and doing it now avoids establishing the raster pattern for a whole family of indicators.",
